Under federal law, a baby can be covered through Familienversicherung (family cover for dependants under statutory insurance). SGB V §10(1) says the children of members of a statutory health insurance fund are insured ("Versichert sind … die Kinder von Mitgliedern") if the child meets all five conditions:
1. lives or habitually resides in Germany;
2. is not insured in their own right under § 5(1) nos. 1 to 8, 11 to 12, and is not voluntarily insured;
3. is not exempt from insurance and not released from compulsory insurance;
4. is not self-employed as their main occupation;
5. has no total income that regularly exceeds one seventh of the monthly reference figure under § 18 SGB IV.

SGB V §10(2) covers children up to their 18th birthday. It extends cover to age 23 if they are not working, and to age 25 if they are in school, training or a voluntary year. It sets no age limit where a disability present during an earlier period of family cover leaves them unable to support themselves.

There is one exclusion to know about. SGB V §10(3) says children are not insured if three things all hold for the parent who is the member's spouse or partner. That parent is not a member of a health insurance fund. Their total income regularly exceeds one twelfth of the annual earnings ceiling ("Jahresarbeitsentgeltgrenze") per month. And their income is regularly higher than the member's. The excerpts do not give the figure for that ceiling.

If both parents can provide the cover, SGB V §10(5) says the member chooses the fund. SGB V §10(6) says the member must report the insured child to the responsible fund, with the details needed for family cover, and must report any later changes. The excerpts do not say what the report must contain or how quickly it must be made.

The birth certificate is one of the documents you need for registering with the health insurance fund. You must register the birth within 1 week at the register office for the place of birth, and that office issues the certificate. If the baby is born in a hospital, birth centre or maternity clinic, the facility notifies the register office.

Whether your baby meets these conditions is not something this product can assess. Your health insurance fund is where the report goes, so that is where to ask about your own child's cover. In many maternity clinics, baby guides (Lotsendienste) also inform parents about benefits and help in practice with filling in applications. They are free and voluntary.
